  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1148 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nA large upper level ridge will remain centered over the Great\nBasin region of the western U.S. through late week. This ridge\nwill extend into the lower MS River Valley, while relatively\nstrong northwest flow aloft extends from southwest Canada into\nthe upper OH River Valley. The Midsouth will remain under the\ninfluence of the upper ridge through late week, resulting in no\nrainfall and high temperatures around 10 degrees above normal.\nThrough Friday, the main concern will remain low relative\nhumidity in the afternoon, which will maintain elevated wildland\nfire danger. For the days 1-3, forecast dewpoints were heavily\nweighted toward CONSShort guidance, with minor input from the\nNBM.\n\nDespite low daytime humidity, a warm front will lift into north\nMS late tonight, then stall prior to daybreak Wednesday. Areas of\nfog will be possible along and ahead of the warm front early in\nWednesday morning. This shallow humid layer will mix out\nWednesday afternoon.\n\nFor the weekend, current attention remains focused on Invest 92L\nin the southwestern Gulf. Latest NHC guidance places an\n80 percent chance of tropical cyclone development in the next\nseven days. 18Z/00Z global models and latest tropical model\nguidance tracks show moderately improved consensus, which lifts\nthe system to the LA/MS coast, followed by a north/northeast\nmovement thereafter. At this early time, the NBM chance PoPs for\nthe weekend appear on track for the Midsouth. Please refer to the\nlatest official NHC guidance regarding Invest 92L.",
